// FRONTIER & PHILOSOPHY VOYAGER  |  L18 — The Simulation Hypothesis
// Age band : voyagers (11-12)   Guide: orion (Phoenix)
// Standards: Coreverse Original — Philosophy, Metaphysics, Epistemology, Philosophy of Mind
// CALIBRATED: Voyager spec v1.1 (May 2026)
//
// SUBJECT NOTE — Frontier & Philosophy:
//   - The simulation hypothesis: the idea that our entire reality might be a computer simulation.
//     Presents the actual argument (the "simulation trilemma" style reasoning) fairly and
//     seriously, AND its genuine problems. Builds on L01 (metaphysics / "what is real"), the
//     knowledge lesson (how do we know?), L05-ish skepticism threads, and L17 (tech futures).
//   - EVENHANDEDNESS: present the argument at its strongest (it's taken seriously by real thinkers)
//     AND its serious weaknesses (no evidence, likely unfalsifiable, assumptions stacked on
//     assumptions, regress). Don't declare it true or false — it's genuinely uncertain, and a big
//     part of the lesson is that it may be UNFALSIFIABLE, which is itself the key teaching.
//   - PROTECTIVE BACKBONE (central here): the unfalsifiability lesson. A fun, untestable idea is
//     fine as speculation, but becomes a problem when (a) treated as established knowledge, (b)
//     used to dismiss reality / consequences ("nothing matters, it's all fake"), or (c) monetized
//     into a belief system or grift. Teach: distinguish fun speculation from knowledge; notice
//     when an idea is built so no evidence could ever count against it; and don't let an
//     unfalsifiable idea reorganize your life or open your wallet. Same clear-eyes spine.
//   - Honesty tier 4 (genuinely open, possibly unanswerable). Em-dashes natural; do NOT replace.
//
// Interaction format: ARGUMENT-BUILDER (screen 7). Audited contract (NOT verified vs shipped file
// in this working dir): {id,type,headline,intro,claim,blocks:[{id,text,type}],modelArgument,
// reflectionPrompt}. block.type in premise|evidence|objection|response|conclusion. Student
// assembles blocks into an argument; no single correct order; modelArgument shows one strong
// assembly. If player reports stuck / no Continue on install, verify field names vs player source.
//
// QUIZ STANDARD (locked): 8 MC + 1 TF + 1 inference. Short ~1-line options, comparable length,
// plausible misconception distractors, correct rotated/not-longest; teaching in `explanation`.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

