          type: `welcome`,
          guideText: `{name}. Today's idea sounds like pure science fiction, and yet it's taken seriously by real philosophers and scientists, and thinking it through will sharpen one of the single most important mental tools you can own. Here it is: what if our entire reality — everything you see, touch, and experience, the whole universe — is actually a computer simulation? What if you are, in effect, a character in an unimaginably advanced video game, and "reality" is running on some vast computer in a world beyond our own? It's a wild idea, and I want to do two things with it today. First, take it genuinely seriously: there's an actual argument for it, it's cleverer than it first sounds, and wrestling with it will stretch your mind in wonderful ways and deepen the questions about reality and knowledge you've explored all band. But second — and this is where the real gold is — I want to use this idea to teach you about a concept that will protect you for the rest of your life: unfalsifiability. Because the simulation hypothesis turns out to be a nearly perfect example of an idea that might be impossible to test, even in principle — and learning to recognize ideas like that, and to handle them wisely, is one of the most powerful clear-thinking skills there is. It's the difference between a fun speculation and a trap. So today we get to do something delightful: take a mind-bending sci-fi idea completely seriously as philosophy, AND extract from it a tool that will help you tell the difference, forever, between a genuinely testable claim and one that's built so that nothing could ever prove it wrong. Let's question reality itself.`,
          headline: `The Simulation Hypothesis`,
